{
 "id": "rate-limiter-log",
 "title": "Sliding-Window Rate Limiter",
 "pattern": "two-pointers",
 "level": "medium",
 "statement": "<p>An API allows each user at most <code>limit</code> requests in any <code>window</code> seconds. You get the requests in arrival order and must decide, one by one, whether to allow each.</p><p>Write <code>def solution(requests, limit, window)</code>. <code>requests</code> is a list of <code>[user, t]</code> pairs: <code>user</code> is a string and <code>t</code> is an integer time in seconds. Times never decrease. A request at time <code>t</code> is <b>allowed</b> if that user has fewer than <code>limit</code> <i>allowed</i> requests with time in <code>(t - window, t]</code> (strictly after <code>t - window</code>, up to and including <code>t</code>). Rejected requests do not count. Users do not affect each other.</p><p>Return a list of booleans, one per request, in order: <code>True</code> for allowed, <code>False</code> for rejected.</p>",
 "examples": [
  {
   "input": "requests = [[\"a\", 1], [\"a\", 2], [\"a\", 3], [\"a\", 4]], limit = 2, window = 3",
   "output": "[True, True, False, True]",
   "why": "At t=3 the window is <code>(0, 3]</code> and already holds the allowed requests at 1 and 2, so it is rejected. At t=4 the window is <code>(1, 4]</code>: only the one at 2 is inside (the rejected one at 3 does not count), so it is allowed."
  },
  {
   "input": "requests = [[\"a\", 0], [\"b\", 0], [\"a\", 0], [\"b\", 1]], limit = 1, window = 10",
   "output": "[True, True, False, False]",
   "why": "Each user gets their own window. <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> each get one request; their second one, inside 10 seconds, is rejected."
  },
  {
   "input": "requests = [[\"u\", 5], [\"u\", 5], [\"u\", 5], [\"u\", 5], [\"u\", 6]], limit = 3, window = 1",
   "output": "[True, True, True, False, True]",
   "why": "A burst at the same second: three get through. At t=6 the window <code>(5, 6]</code> no longer includes t=5, so the user is clear again."
  }
 ],
 "constraints": [
  "0 &le; len(requests) &le; 100,000",
  "0 &le; limit &le; 100,000",
  "1 &le; window &le; 1,000,000,000",
  "0 &le; t &le; 1,000,000,000, and times never decrease",
  "up to 10,000 distinct users"
 ],
 "starter": "def solution(requests, limit, window):\n    # write your code here\n    pass\n",
 "think": [
  {
   "step": "Understand",
   "q": "What do you need to know about a user to judge their next request?",
   "a": "Only the times of their <i>allowed</i> requests that are still inside the window. Older ones and rejected ones never matter again."
  },
  {
   "step": "Small example by hand",
   "q": "<code>limit = 2, window = 3</code>, user <code>a</code> at times 1, 2, 3, 4.",
   "a": "t=1: log [] &rarr; allow, log [1]. t=2: allow, log [1, 2]. t=3: drop times &le; 0 (none); 2 in the log, reject. t=4: drop times &le; 1, log [2]; allow, log [2, 4]."
  },
  {
   "step": "Naive idea",
   "q": "What is the first thing you would write?",
   "a": "Keep every allowed time per user and, for each request, count how many are in <code>(t - window, t]</code>. Correct, but each request rescans the whole history: <code>O(n&sup2;)</code> for a busy user."
  },
  {
   "step": "Which pattern?",
   "q": "Clue words?",
   "a": "\"in any window of\", \"times never decrease\" &rarr; <b>sliding window</b>. Because time only moves forward, a timestamp that falls out of the window never comes back. Keep each user's allowed times in a <code>deque</code>: drop from the left while they are too old, append on the right. Each time enters and leaves once."
  },
  {
   "step": "Plan in plain steps",
   "q": "Write the steps.",
   "a": "<ol><li><code>logs = defaultdict(deque)</code>.</li><li>For each <code>[user, t]</code>: while <code>log[0] &lt;= t - window</code>, <code>popleft()</code>.</li><li>If <code>len(log) &lt; limit</code>: append <code>t</code>, output <code>True</code>.</li><li>Else output <code>False</code> and do <b>not</b> append.</li></ol>"
  },
  {
   "step": "Edge cases to test",
   "q": "What could break?",
   "a": "A request exactly <code>window</code> seconds after an old one (the old one is out: the interval is open on the left). Many requests at the same second. <code>limit = 0</code> (reject all). Rejected requests sneaking into the log. Users with the same prefix (<code>\"a\"</code> vs <code>\"ab\"</code>). No requests."
  }
 ],
 "hints": [
  "Only allowed requests count. Do not add rejected ones to anything.",
  "Times never go down, so once a timestamp is older than <code>t - window</code> you can throw it away for good.",
  "Per user, keep a <code>collections.deque</code> of allowed times. Pop from the left while <code>log[0] &lt;= t - window</code>; then compare <code>len(log)</code> with <code>limit</code>."
 ],
 "solution": "from collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef solution(requests, limit, window):\n    logs = defaultdict(deque)       # user -> times of ALLOWED requests still in the window\n    out = []\n    for user, t in requests:\n        log = logs[user]\n        # The window is (t - window, t]: anything at or before t - window has expired.\n        while log and log[0] <= t - window:\n            log.popleft()\n        if len(log) < limit:\n            log.append(t)           # only allowed requests are logged\n            out.append(True)\n        else:\n            out.append(False)\n    return out\n",
 "brute": "def solution(requests, limit, window):\n    # Keep every allowed time forever and recount the window each time: O(n^2)\n    allowed = {}\n    out = []\n    for user, t in requests:\n        times = allowed.setdefault(user, [])\n        recent = 0\n        for s in times:\n            if t - window < s <= t:\n                recent += 1\n        if recent < limit:\n            times.append(t)\n            out.append(True)\n        else:\n            out.append(False)\n    return out\n",
 "explain": "<p>This is the <b>sliding window log</b> algorithm. Each user has a queue of the times of their allowed requests. Before judging a request, drop the times that have slid out of the window; then the queue length is exactly the number of allowed requests in <code>(t - window, t]</code>.</p><ul><li><code>limit = 2, window = 3</code>. t=1: queue [] &rarr; allow &rarr; [1].</li><li>t=2: nothing expires (1 &gt; -1) &rarr; allow &rarr; [1, 2].</li><li>t=3: expire times &le; 0 (none), length 2 = limit &rarr; reject. The queue stays [1, 2].</li><li>t=4: expire times &le; 1 &rarr; [2], length 1 &rarr; allow &rarr; [2, 4].</li></ul><p>Every timestamp is appended once and popped at most once, so the total work is <code>O(n)</code>. The cost is memory: up to <code>limit</code> timestamps per active user. That is why real systems often switch to a token bucket or fixed-window counters, which keep a constant amount of state per user.</p>",
 "complexity": "O(n) time overall (each allowed time is pushed and popped once), O(users &times; limit) extra space",
