  "round": "live",
  "tag": "inspired",
  "source": "NodeFlair: GovTech \"design a rate limiter API\"",
  "evidence": "NodeFlair (search snippet, unverified) lists \"design a rate limiter API\" for GovTech as a design question; this is the coding core an interviewer could ask you to write live, not a reported coding problem.",
  "extensions": [
   "Switch to a <b>token bucket</b>: capacity <code>limit</code>, refilled at <code>limit / window</code> tokens per second. You keep 2 numbers per user instead of up to <code>limit</code> timestamps, and short bursts are allowed.",
   "Use <b>fixed-window counters</b> (<code>count[user, t // window]</code>). Show the boundary burst: with limit 100 per minute, 100 requests at 0:59 and 100 at 1:00 all pass, 200 in 2 seconds. A sliding-window counter (weighted mix of the current and previous window) fixes most of it.",
   "Run the limiter across 10 API servers. Keep counters in Redis with <code>INCR</code> + <code>EXPIRE</code>, or a sorted set per user for the log, and do the check-and-add in one Lua script so two servers cannot both see \"99\" and both allow.",
   "Return HTTP 429 with a <code>Retry-After</code> header. How do you compute the seconds until the oldest logged request expires?"
  ]
